#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
# aurora-disk-probe — read-only survey of the disks for « Installer à côté de … » (install alongside).
#
# Run as root (blkid -p, ntfsresize --info, dumpe2fs, resize2fs -P and debugfs read the partitions):
#     sudo -n /usr/lib/aurora/helpers/aurora-disk-probe [--disk /dev/sda ...]
#     aurora-disk-probe --ui            (= aurora-install --probe --json: one line {"probe":{firmware, disks}}, sizes in
#                                        bytes, partitions with type/state/used/min/esp — the shape installer.js reads)
#     aurora-disk-probe --disk DEV --plan free:STARTMIB:ENDMIB|shrink:N:NEWSIZEMIB[:SELENEMIB]|reuse:N --fingerprint HEX
#                       [--esp N] [--format json|shell]
#     aurora-disk-probe --chainload ESPDIR --esp-partuuid GUID [--efibootmgr FILE] [--name NAME] [--os-id ID]
#                       (no root, reads only ESPDIR and FILE: the other systems' own EFI boot loaders, see chainload_entries)
#
# Nothing is ever written: no file system is mounted read-write (the EFI system partition is mounted
# read-only, or read with mtools where the kernel has no FAT driver), ext4 is read with debugfs/dumpe2fs,
# NTFS with ntfsresize --info / ntfsls / ntfscat (all read-only), and os-prober is NOT run here (it mounts
# partitions, which would replay an unclean ext4 journal).
#
# Survey (default): one JSON document on stdout:
#   { version: 1, firmware: 'uefi'|'bios', minSeleneMiB, suggestedSeleneMiB, disks: [disk] }
#   disk: { path, name, model, size (bytes), sizeMiB, transport, removable, table: 'gpt'|'dos'|null, sectorSize,
#           fingerprint (sha256 of `sfdisk --dump`), partitions: [part], free: [{startMiB, endMiB, sizeMiB}],
#           esp: {number, path, sizeMiB, freeMiB, folders, hasFallback, ubuntuFolder, readable} | null,
#           systems: [{name, kind: 'windows'|'linux'|'other', partition}], bitlocker, warnings: [code],
#           chain: {name, id, uuid, partition} | null   (the Linux system whose boot loader is on the ESP: its
#                  os-release PRETTY_NAME, the partition whose /etc/fstab mounts the ESP, else the only Linux one),
#           alongside: { possible, reason, message, plans: [plan] } }
#   part: { number, path, start, sectors, startMiB, sizeMiB, type, name, fstype, label, uuid, mounted, esp,
#           usedMiB, os, flags: [bitlocker|hibernated|dirty|luks|lvm|needs-recovery|mounted|in-use|swap],
#           shrink: { possible, reason, message, minSizeMiB, maxSizeMiB, suggestedSizeMiB, spaceMiB } }
#   plan: { kind: 'free', startMiB, endMiB, sizeMiB }
#       | { kind: 'shrink', partition, path, fstype, label, os, currentSizeMiB, minSizeMiB, maxSizeMiB,
#           suggestedSizeMiB, spaceMiB (current size + free space right after it) }
#       | { kind: 'reuse', partition, path, startMiB, sizeMiB }: the « Selene OS » partition an installation that
#           stopped left behind (GPT name « Selene OS », ext4 « AURORA » or not formatted yet, at least 20 GB, and no
#           AURORA_INSTALL_COMPLETE in its /etc/aurora/install-info): formatted again, nothing else changes. A finished
#           Selene OS installation is never offered (it is a system, and its EFI/ubuntu folder refuses the disk).
#   alongside.partition: when no plan is possible because of one partition (e.g. hibernated Windows), its number
#   reason codes (alongside / shrink): bios, no-table, mbr, no-esp, esp-unreadable, esp-full, ubuntu-folder,
#     no-space, in-use; btrfs, xfs, luks, lvm, bitlocker, hibernated, dirty, needs-recovery, unsupported,
#     too-full, mounted, unreadable
#
# Plan check (--plan, used by aurora-install --alongside before anything is written): the plan is checked
# against a fresh survey of DEV (same rules as above) and the partition table fingerprint; prints either
#   OK=1 and the exact sectors (--format shell: KEY=VALUE lines) or OK=0 CODE=… MESSAGE=…
# Exit codes: 0 ok (survey, or a valid plan), 2 invalid arguments, 3 not found, 4 plan refused.
#
# Test aid: --assume-uefi treats the running firmware as UEFI (build machines without /sys/firmware/efi;
# aurora-install passes it only when AURORA_INSTALL_TEST_NO_FIRMWARE=1, which sudo never forwards).
